  "question": {
    "text": "<p>An electric dipole is formed by two charges&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>-</mo><mi>q</mi></math> located in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mi>y</mi></math>-plane at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mm</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#8722;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mm</mi></math>, respectively, as shown in the figure. The electric potential at point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mm</mi></math> due to the dipole is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math>. The charges <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8211;</mo><mi>q</mi></math> are then moved to the points <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mo>&#8722;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mm</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#8722;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mm</mi></math>, respectively. What is the value of electric potential at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math>&nbsp;due to the new dipole?</p><p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_advanced/89918752-cf37-4a23-9c23-ac6df765182c.png\" style=\"width: 400px; height: 273px;\" /></p>",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
